    "global": {
      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "With both coefficients free, a unique finite MLE exists for every nonconstant finite sample. Constant samples have unbounded likelihood. With location held, a unique positive scale MLE exists unless every observation equals that location. With positive scale held, the location MLE is unique even for constant data. Holding both coefficients leaves likelihood evaluation.",
      "reason": "Profiling location gives $\\mu(\\sigma)=-\\sigma\\log(n^{-1}\\sum_i e^{-x_i/\\sigma})$. The scale equation is $F(\\sigma)=\\sigma+E_\\sigma[x]-\\bar x=0$, with weights proportional to $e^{-x_i/\\sigma}$. Its derivative is $1+\\operatorname{Var}_\\sigma(x)/\\sigma^2>0$. For nonconstant data, its limit at zero is $\\min_i x_i-\\bar x<0$, and it tends to infinity with scale. The profile score has sign $-F$, giving a unique maximum. With location held, put $t=1/\\sigma$ and $d_i=x_i-\\mu$. The inverse-scale score $n/t-\\sum_i d_i(1-e^{-td_i})$ has derivative $-n/t^2-\\sum_i d_i^2e^{-td_i}<0$ and crosses zero whenever some $d_i\\ne0$. All-zero residuals instead give unbounded collapse."
